  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sida Fair Finance Brazil 24 27 IDEC</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The FF coalition in Brazil has been in operation since 2014 and published its latest assessment of the 8 biggest banks in Brazil inDecember 2022. Whereas in the beginning the coalition was ignored by the biggest banks of Brazil, by now the FF coalition has an ongoing dialogue with all the major banks in Brazil, resulting in policy and practice changes.
Fair Finance Brazil (GBR Guia dos Bancos Respons&#xe1;veis91) targets commercial banks and one public bank (BNDES) and focuses on: (1) pressuring banks to adopt better practices; (2) influencing regulators to hold banks accountable for the socio-environmental impacts they produce directly and indirectly; and (3) mobilizing consumers of financial services to demand their rights. GBR adds value to the project through its long experience in consumer protection guidelines, financial inclusion, fair digital economy and in the fight against the practices of financial institutions that lead to over-indebtedness of the population.
GBR will publish case studies on consumer rights, digital economy, biodiversity, human rights, and indigenous rights. The coalition will publish 2 Policy Assessments (2024 and 2026), covering the 8 biggestcommercial banks in Brazil.
Additionally, FF Brazil aims to support other coalitions in Latin America in developing their projects,by providing assistance through training, sharing experiences, and fostering ongoing dialogue. FF Brazil aims to facilitate at least one dialogue between a South American community affected by a company financed by a Brazilian bank, such as Ita&#xfa;.
Fair Finance Brazil consists of IDEC, Conectas, Sou da Paz, Oxfam Brasil and World Animal Protection (WAP).
As of 1st January 2025, all funding to IDEC is channeled through Oxfam Brazil.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">IDEC - Instituto Brasileiro de Defe</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">IDEC, the Brazilian institute for consumer interests, was founded in 1987. It is a civic organisation without profit motives or political preferences, and independent of economic interests. Oxfam Novib has been supporting IDEC since 1998. Some of the results it has achieved in recent years are safer condoms for men, a ban on genetically manipulated foods, withdrawal from the market of more than 100 kinds of antibiotics, legal aid against excessive increases in health insurance premiums, and a substantialreduction of the costs of reconnecting water.
Together with the Dutch Consumer Union Oxfam Novib has also supported an improvement of IDECs services to its members and the public.
Since 2014, IDEC is the lead of the Brazilian coalition of the Fair Finance Guide. Main themes are thetransparancy of the Brazilian banks in international perspective, the integration of ESG criteria in bank policies and practices and the socio-environmental effects of bank credits to specific sectors like agro industry, mining and power generation. The BrazilianFFG gives specific attention to Consumer Protection and Financial Inclusion issues. The website of the Guia dos Bancos Respons&#xe1;veisgive an annual update of the assessment of the sustainability of the banks&apos; policies.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
